WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.103
You now have the goal defined
and the file specified.

2
00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:05.171
The next step is to decide on

3
00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:06.773
the graph model for your structured data,

4
00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:11.678
meaning what types of nodes
and edges will constitute the domain graph.

5
00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:14.981
You'll set up a loop of sub-agents
that will refine the model.

6
00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:17.450
Let's dive in.

7
00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.786
You're working through the Structured Data Agent, through

8
00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:22.155
the workflow that started with the user intent,

9
00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:24.858
and then file suggestions,
and finally we're ready to make a proposal

10
00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:28.161
for what the graph schema might look like.

11
00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:30.563
That's the job of this agent.

12
00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:32.465
Now this agent also introduces some new ideas

13
00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:34.334
in terms of how you compose an agent.

14
00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:37.470
This agent actually has multiple agents inside of it

15
00:00:38.171 --> 00:00:41.474
where particularly, there's one agent that makes a proposal.

16
00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:43.410
So it says here's what I

17
00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:44.878
think we can make for a graph.

18
00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:48.081
And then another agent takes a look at that and criticizes it.

19
00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:52.485
So this is a critic pattern,
very common through many multi-agent systems.

20
00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:54.954
So in the top level coordinator, it has only

21
00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:57.123
a couple of tools that it's working with here.

22
00:00:57.524 --> 00:01:00.427
It has one tool that we're calling the refinement_loop_as_tool.

23
00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:04.230
And then two kind of normal tools,
the get_proposed_construction_plan

24
00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.433
and also approve_the_proposed_construction_plan.

25
00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:09.002
Those are similar to the file suggestion

26
00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:11.104
and file approval tools we saw before.

27
00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:14.107
But this refinement_loop_as_tool is interesting.

28
00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:16.209
That actually is an agent itself,

29
00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:18.511
or actually a couple of agents

30
00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:20.613
working together. Let's look inside of that.

31
00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:25.118
So the refinement loop is actually an agent
that helps coordinate sub-agents.

32
00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:28.421
The LoopAgent here has three sub-agents:

33
00:01:28.721 --> 00:01:31.591
schema_proposal_agent, schema_critic_agent,

34
00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:35.395
and then something that we're calling the
CheckStatusAndEscalate agent.

35
00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:38.798
These work together looping over and over time

36
00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:40.900
until they get to a final result.

37
00:01:41.134 --> 00:01:43.636
The schema_proposal_agent makes the proposal,

38
00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:45.939
the schema_critic_agent criticizes it,

39
00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:49.309
and then the CheckStatusAndEscalate agent is responsible for

40
00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:51.911
looking at the output from the critic and deciding,

41
00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:54.314
is the critic satisfied or do we have to try again?

42
00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:56.549
And if the critic is satisfied, it's

43
00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:59.119
this agent that actually makes the escalation happen

44
00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:03.223
so that this LoopAgent terminates.
Now, this could go on forever,

45
00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:05.725
so that when you call the LoopAgent,

46
00:02:05.725 --> 00:02:07.060
we can also set a limit to

47
00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:08.628
how many iterations through the loop we'll try.

48
00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:11.030
If we fail to get to some kind of consensus,

49
00:02:11.231 --> 00:02:12.932
then we're going to kick back to the user

50
00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:14.634
saying, hey, we're not sure what to do here.

51
00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:17.237
The top-level coordinator will then be responsible

52
00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:19.305
for actually getting some more input from the user.

53
00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:21.908
You'll start with the usual imports.

54
00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:24.210
When those are ready, you can define

55
00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.212
the LLM that we're going to use

56
00:02:26.513 --> 00:02:29.215
and check it out. Great.

57
00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:30.917
With all that ready, we can

58
00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:32.519
go ahead and start our tool definitions.

59
00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:35.522
You can start with the agent instructions.

60
00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:39.058
Now the agents we're going to be looking
at more closely here

61
00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:41.361
are the Schema Proposal Agent and then the critic.

62
00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:43.029
So let's take a look at the

63
00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:44.364
instructions we're going to give to those.

64
00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:48.368
So for the proposal agent, for its role and its goal,

65
00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:50.870
we're going to tell it that it's an expert

66
00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.672
at graph data modeling with property graphs.

67
00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:54.941
And you can look at the detail

68
00:02:54.941 --> 00:02:56.643
here about where it's describing to it.

69
00:02:57.043 --> 00:03:00.713
But the novel part here really in this part of the prompt

70
00:03:00.914 --> 00:03:03.316
is that we're going to be injecting
here some feedback.

71
00:03:03.716 --> 00:03:06.085
Now the feedback initially will be empty,

72
00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:08.254
and so we're telling the agent that

73
00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:10.623
consider the feedback only if it is available.

74
00:03:10.924 --> 00:03:12.759
But because it can be empty, we don't want to

75
00:03:12.759 --> 00:03:14.627
be confused about the rest of the prompt being part

76
00:03:15.094 --> 00:03:17.330
for the feedback. So we enclose it

77
00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.599
in some XML here, some pseudo XML,

78
00:03:19.899 --> 00:03:22.235
saying here's the feedback and then here's the

79
00:03:22.235 --> 00:03:24.871
closing feedback
because most LLMs understand that pretty well

80
00:03:24.871 --> 00:03:28.575
like this is a a delimiter. So we have a delimiter here

81
00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:33.079
and then this curly braces feedback ends up being a template

82
00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:36.983
variable that gets injected by Google ADK.

83
00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.052
Google ADK when it's assembling the prompt,

84
00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:41.054
we'll look for these kinds of parts of the text

85
00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:43.823
and replace this with context,

86
00:03:43.823 --> 00:03:47.026
a context variable. So if in the tool context state,

87
00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:50.830
if the state has a feedback variable
that's defined in the dictionary,

88
00:03:51.231 --> 00:03:53.233
the value of that will get injected here.

89
00:03:53.833 --> 00:03:55.835
So if there's feedback in the context,

90
00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:58.238
that'll get passed into this part of the prompt.

91
00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:01.808
Okay. You've given the agent a goal

92
00:04:01.808 --> 00:04:03.776
and also a role.

93
00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:05.478
And now we're going to give it some

94
00:04:05.478 --> 00:04:07.080
hints about how to go about its job.

95
00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:11.084
Now, the hints here are lengthier
than anything you've done so far.

96
00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:14.487
The reason for that is that most LLMs

97
00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.623
and OpenAI's included

98
00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:19.325
are pretty good at doing graph data modeling,

99
00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:21.327
but they're not exceptional at it.

100
00:04:21.327 --> 00:04:23.730
And so we're going to give it
some extra guidance here

101
00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:27.133
about thinking how to work with
the data files that are available

102
00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:29.736
and what those data files might look like as a graph.

103
00:04:30.036 --> 00:04:32.839
You can look through all the guidance here
and notice that

104
00:04:32.839 --> 00:04:35.341
we've got best practices for knowledge graph

105
00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:37.844
construction really encoded inside of this prompt.

106
00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:40.947
So overall, every file in the approved file list

107
00:04:40.947 --> 00:04:43.249
should somehow become part of the graph.

108
00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.853
And within those files, these are CSV files,

109
00:04:46.853 --> 00:04:48.655
often you'll see unique identifiers.

110
00:04:48.655 --> 00:04:51.557
We tell it to take a look for those unique identifiers,

111
00:04:51.557 --> 00:04:53.860
try to identify what those identifiers are,

112
00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.562
and use those for actually understanding

113
00:04:56.562 --> 00:04:59.232
the role of that particular file
in constructing the graph.

114
00:04:59.666 --> 00:05:01.668
Then we give it some guidance,

115
00:05:01.668 --> 00:05:04.170
basically some design rules for how to think about

116
00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:07.173
whether a particular file might represent a node

117
00:05:07.173 --> 00:05:10.476
or might represent a relationship.
And you can look through the particulars,

118
00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:12.378
but there's like often some semantic hints

119
00:05:12.378 --> 00:05:14.280
in the naming of the file itself,

120
00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.482
the number of identifiers that appear,

121
00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:19.686
whether those identifiers are actually unique to the file

122
00:05:19.686 --> 00:05:21.587
or that they're unique to some other file.

123
00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:24.657
all these kinds of things are what you or I might do

124
00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:26.626
when we're just performing as data engineers

125
00:05:26.626 --> 00:05:28.928
and we've been handed a bunch of data files.

126
00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:30.630
We basically do this with grep, with cat,

127
00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.398
maybe we throw it into an editor

128
00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:34.133
and take a look at the files.

129
00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:36.135
This is the same kind of thing we would do.

130
00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:38.938
Now actually articulated here inside of the prompt

131
00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:41.341
so helping the agent figure out how to go about doing

132
00:05:41.341 --> 00:05:44.143
the same kind of data modeling that we already know.

133
00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:48.014
So breaking it down whether
this is node or a relationship,

134
00:05:48.114 --> 00:05:50.416
particular, you know, design rules for nodes,

135
00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:54.120
often have a single identifier, not multiple identifiers,

136
00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.422
but if the file does have multiple identifiers,

137
00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:59.625
the extra identifiers might be reference relationships.

138
00:06:00.226 --> 00:06:02.362
So this is what happens when in a

139
00:06:02.362 --> 00:06:04.497
relational database you might have foreign key relationships.

140
00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:07.100
In a graph, those will turn into relationships.

141
00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.704
Then for relationships themselves,
there's two ways that they really show up.

142
00:06:12.004 --> 00:06:14.173
There's either what we're calling here

143
00:06:14.173 --> 00:06:16.709
a full relationship and a reference relationship.

144
00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:19.011
And then we break that down even further.

145
00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:20.713
We describe to the LLM what

146
00:06:20.713 --> 00:06:22.715
do we mean by a full relationship

147
00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:25.118
and what do we mean by a reference relationship.

148
00:06:25.618 --> 00:06:29.522
There's two components here we're describing
how to identify what they are,

149
00:06:29.522 --> 00:06:31.257
but then also having identified them,

150
00:06:31.257 --> 00:06:33.292
how to turn those into a graph.

151
00:06:33.826 --> 00:06:37.630
Finally, after we've given it all kinds of design guidance,

152
00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:41.868
we recommend that the resulting schema should be
a properly connected graph.

153
00:06:41.868 --> 00:06:43.503
This is important because it's so

154
00:06:43.503 --> 00:06:45.371
obviously entirely possible that you can import

155
00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:48.074
all kinds of data and not have it be connected.

156
00:06:48.074 --> 00:06:49.575
It's not really much of a graph,

157
00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:51.077
it's not very useful at that point.

158
00:06:51.077 --> 00:06:52.678
So it should be totally connected.

159
00:06:52.678 --> 00:06:54.881
If there are any isolated components,

160
00:06:54.881 --> 00:06:57.683
that's probably a problem and something that should be considered.

161
00:06:58.184 --> 00:07:00.987
You can then give the agent some chain of thought directions.

162
00:07:01.287 --> 00:07:03.990
This is also a bit more elaborate than we've done before.

163
00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:06.492
You can look through this one step at a time.

164
00:07:06.893 --> 00:07:08.494
You'll start with actually getting the

165
00:07:08.494 --> 00:07:10.396
agent to prepare for the task ahead.

166
00:07:10.797 --> 00:07:12.532
We know that this task is based

167
00:07:12.532 --> 00:07:14.300
on what the established user goal is,

168
00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.402
the list of approved files,

169
00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:18.171
and also whether or not there's an

170
00:07:18.171 --> 00:07:19.839
approved construction plan or a current one.

171
00:07:19.839 --> 00:07:22.241
construction plan. So even proposing it,

172
00:07:22.241 --> 00:07:24.043
this might not be the first time

173
00:07:24.043 --> 00:07:27.647
that this agent has actually proposed a construction plan.

174
00:07:27.647 --> 00:07:29.849
And so if one already exists, it's possible for it

175
00:07:29.849 --> 00:07:32.351
to actually get that current construction plan

176
00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:34.854
and then decide whether it needs to make any changes to it.

177
00:07:35.421 --> 00:07:37.523
The actual chain of thought then

178
00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:41.327
is as usual, encouraging it to think carefully,
perform step-by-step

179
00:07:41.327 --> 00:07:44.130
using tools that are available for any actions.

180
00:07:44.597 --> 00:07:46.399
It has to go through each approved file,

181
00:07:46.399 --> 00:07:48.801
considering whether it is a node or a relationship.

182
00:07:48.801 --> 00:07:51.103
This is echoing our design guidance from earlier.

183
00:07:51.904 --> 00:07:53.606
And then every time it finds an identifier

184
00:07:53.639 --> 00:07:56.542
or it thinks that it's found an identifier,

185
00:07:56.642 --> 00:07:59.745
it should verify that it has found an unique identifier

186
00:07:59.846 --> 00:08:02.048
by using a tool called the search_file tool.

187
00:08:02.148 --> 00:08:04.750
This is basically a Python-based version of grep

188
00:08:04.851 --> 00:08:07.053
where the things that it has found an identifier,

189
00:08:07.153 --> 00:08:09.555
it can try to verify that that thing is actually unique

190
00:08:09.655 --> 00:08:11.924
by looking for whether there are multiple
occurrences of it.

191
00:08:12.458 --> 00:08:14.460
in the same file.

192
00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.529
We then encourage it to actually take into

193
00:08:16.529 --> 00:08:18.464
account the guidance that we gave it earlier

194
00:08:18.464 --> 00:08:20.867
for deciding whether that is in fact a node
or a relationship.

195
00:08:21.167 --> 00:08:24.770
Then for a node file, it has a special tool for proposing

196
00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:26.772
how to take that node file and

197
00:08:26.772 --> 00:08:28.774
perform what we're calling here a construction.

198
00:08:29.775 --> 00:08:32.612
special tool where it can record for a given file

199
00:08:32.879 --> 00:08:34.647
the construction of how to take that file

200
00:08:34.647 --> 00:08:36.649
and turn it into a node within the graph.

201
00:08:37.383 --> 00:08:39.785
Similarly, if it thinks it's found a relationship,

202
00:08:39.785 --> 00:08:42.588
there's another tool that it can call called

203
00:08:42.588 --> 00:08:44.590
the proposed relationship construction tool

204
00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:47.293
that can be used for proposing a relationship construction.

205
00:08:47.593 --> 00:08:50.696
using those two tools, it'll go through every file

206
00:08:51.097 --> 00:08:52.532
and whether it's a node or a

207
00:08:52.532 --> 00:08:54.000
relationship, it'll call one of those tools

208
00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:55.801
to create a construction rule for turning

209
00:08:55.801 --> 00:08:57.503
that file into part of the graph.

210
00:08:58.204 --> 00:09:01.340
And if needed, it can also remove any construction rules.

211
00:09:02.074 --> 00:09:05.077
Once it's done, it'll use
the get_proposed_construction_plan

212
00:09:05.077 --> 00:09:07.680
tool to present the final plan to the user.

213
00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:10.182
This get_proposed_construction_plan tool

214
00:09:10.283 --> 00:09:12.184
will have the full list of all

215
00:09:12.184 --> 00:09:14.086
the individual construction rules in one set.

216
00:09:15.621 --> 00:09:17.356
You can then combine all this together

217
00:09:17.356 --> 00:09:19.725
to the final instructions
we're going to give to the agent.

218
00:09:25.364 --> 00:09:27.466
You can now move on to tool definitions.

219
00:09:27.767 --> 00:09:30.970
There are many common tools
that we've defined in previous notebooks,

220
00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:33.072
so we'll just go ahead and import those,

221
00:09:33.372 --> 00:09:37.877
like the get_approved_user_goal,
get_approved_files, and also the sample_file utility.

222
00:09:38.311 --> 00:09:41.914
The agent's instructions also talked about a search file tool.

223
00:09:42.515 --> 00:09:45.117
That search file tool is basically
a grep-like function

224
00:09:45.318 --> 00:09:47.086
that can read a file, look through

225
00:09:47.086 --> 00:09:49.088
a couple of the lines of the file

226
00:09:49.488 --> 00:09:51.390
and look for patterns. It's not a

227
00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:53.292
very sophisticated query the way grep is,

228
00:09:53.492 --> 00:09:55.094
but it's enough to allow the LLM to actually

229
00:09:55.127 --> 00:09:57.463
look for things that might occur. For instance,

230
00:09:57.463 --> 00:09:59.231
looking for unique identifiers.

231
00:09:59.532 --> 00:10:01.334
You could pass that in as a constant here,

232
00:10:01.334 --> 00:10:04.036
and then look through the file to see if that constant occurs.

233
00:10:04.337 --> 00:10:06.939
The result of this tool will actually be the number of lines

234
00:10:06.939 --> 00:10:09.942
and also which lines have that content.

235
00:10:13.779 --> 00:10:17.183
The next tool you'll define is the Propose Node Construction.

236
00:10:17.783 --> 00:10:21.087
Now, this proposes how to take a source data file,

237
00:10:21.387 --> 00:10:23.990
so we're passing in an approved file, the file path.

238
00:10:24.590 --> 00:10:26.525
And then based on that file path, we want to

239
00:10:26.525 --> 00:10:28.494
be able to create a node inside of the graph.

240
00:10:29.095 --> 00:10:31.597
So we describe what the node should look like.

241
00:10:32.398 --> 00:10:35.501
You describe what the node looks like
with a few different things.

242
00:10:35.701 --> 00:10:36.936
One is what is the label

243
00:10:36.936 --> 00:10:38.404
that we should apply to that node?

244
00:10:38.704 --> 00:10:40.806
Labels generally apply to nodes

245
00:10:40.906 --> 00:10:42.274
in ways that describe whether it's

246
00:10:42.274 --> 00:10:43.909
a person, a place, or a thing.

247
00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:46.512
It describes the kind of thing that the node represents.

248
00:10:46.946 --> 00:10:49.248
In addition to that, we identify what the

249
00:10:49.248 --> 00:10:52.752
unique_column_name is so that on the import CSV file,

250
00:10:52.752 --> 00:10:54.954
one of the columns should be a unique column.

251
00:10:54.954 --> 00:10:57.757
If we have identified that, that'll get passed in here.

252
00:10:57.757 --> 00:11:00.760
And then finally, you may not accept all of the columns

253
00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:03.262
as being valid for actually constructing the node.

254
00:11:03.262 --> 00:11:05.965
So the proposed_properties actually describes

255
00:11:05.965 --> 00:11:07.867
from the CSV file, which of the

256
00:11:07.867 --> 00:11:09.769
columns that we actually want to import

257
00:11:09.769 --> 00:11:11.671
and create as properties on the node.

258
00:11:12.772 --> 00:11:14.473
If you look at the actual

259
00:11:14.473 --> 00:11:16.475
implementation of this function,
it's pretty straightforward.

260
00:11:16.876 --> 00:11:18.778
It does some sanity checking as usual.

261
00:11:19.278 --> 00:11:21.180
If we made it through that sanity check,

262
00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:23.616
we then go ahead and describe the

263
00:11:23.616 --> 00:11:25.651
construction rule or construct
the construction rule

264
00:11:25.951 --> 00:11:29.155
based on the input arguments.
This is really just

265
00:11:29.288 --> 00:11:32.158
like a factory function for describing a data object.

266
00:11:32.558 --> 00:11:35.161
So the data object here will be a construction rule

267
00:11:35.361 --> 00:11:37.463
with the type of construction is a node.

268
00:11:37.863 --> 00:11:40.032
It's being derived from a source file that

269
00:11:40.032 --> 00:11:42.468
has been passed in. It's being given a label.

270
00:11:42.868 --> 00:11:45.671
It has a unique_column_name and
also a set of properties.

271
00:11:45.871 --> 00:11:47.973
With that all created,

272
00:11:47.973 --> 00:11:50.976
we then add that to the list of construction rules that exist.

273
00:11:51.677 --> 00:11:55.681
And to be clear, we've just before grabbed
the current construction plan

274
00:11:55.681 --> 00:11:58.684
from the memory. And if it doesn't exist,

275
00:11:58.684 --> 00:12:00.686
this will just be an empty dictionary.

276
00:12:01.087 --> 00:12:03.389
But if it does exist, what do we add

277
00:12:03.556 --> 00:12:06.358
it here. We're going to add it using the label,

278
00:12:06.358 --> 00:12:07.526
cuz all the labels should be

279
00:12:07.526 --> 00:12:08.861
unique, using the label as a key.

280
00:12:08.861 --> 00:12:11.063
We're going to go ahead and add that construction rule.

281
00:12:11.063 --> 00:12:12.765
So that the overall construction plan

282
00:12:12.765 --> 00:12:14.767
will be a set of unique keys,

283
00:12:14.767 --> 00:12:17.369
where each of those keys will be a rule

284
00:12:17.369 --> 00:12:20.372
that is either a node or relationship construction
with the needed information.

285
00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:25.845
You can define a similar tool
for creating relationship construction rules.

286
00:12:26.746 --> 00:12:28.314
Now, if you take a look

287
00:12:28.314 --> 00:12:30.149
at the arguments being passed in here,

288
00:12:30.149 --> 00:12:33.018
they're unique to relationships.
Similar to a node,

289
00:12:33.018 --> 00:12:35.221
it starts with a file name that is

290
00:12:35.221 --> 00:12:37.423
where the relationship is going to be pulled from.

291
00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:40.292
relationships instead of having labels, they have types.

292
00:12:40.693 --> 00:12:43.095
It's going to pass in a proposed relationship type.

293
00:12:43.696 --> 00:12:46.198
And because relationships connect to other nodes,

294
00:12:46.599 --> 00:12:48.601
you want to know what is the label of the node

295
00:12:48.701 --> 00:12:50.803
that this relationship is going to be from

296
00:12:51.303 --> 00:12:54.006
and what is it connecting to. So you get a label

297
00:12:54.573 --> 00:12:57.176
for the from node and a label for the two node.

298
00:12:57.576 --> 00:12:59.745
And then for the from node and the two node,

299
00:12:59.845 --> 00:13:02.047
you also want to know what's unique about that.

300
00:13:02.248 --> 00:13:03.649
So in the CSV file that

301
00:13:03.649 --> 00:13:05.251
is being used to create the construction

302
00:13:05.851 --> 00:13:07.453
for the relationship.

303
00:13:07.653 --> 00:13:10.556
There should be a column that identifies for the from node

304
00:13:10.656 --> 00:13:14.160
what is the unique identifier for that.
So the from_node_column.

305
00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:17.563
There's also a to_node_column
that should have the unique identifiers

306
00:13:17.663 --> 00:13:19.832
for the nodes that the relationship is connecting to.

307
00:13:20.232 --> 00:13:22.434
With all that information,

308
00:13:22.434 --> 00:13:24.036
you can then construct a relationship.

309
00:13:25.037 --> 00:13:27.039
As with the node implementation,

310
00:13:27.239 --> 00:13:29.742
if both the from and the two nodes exist,

311
00:13:30.042 --> 00:13:32.244
then we can go ahead and create the construction rule itself.

312
00:13:33.045 --> 00:13:35.347
And this construction rule will again be a dictionary.

313
00:13:35.648 --> 00:13:38.450
The type of the dictionary will be that it's a relationship,

314
00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:40.619
so this is a relationship construction rule.

315
00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:43.022
It has a source file, relationship type,

316
00:13:43.422 --> 00:13:45.224
a from node, a two node,

317
00:13:45.224 --> 00:13:46.725
and then a set of properties that

318
00:13:46.725 --> 00:13:48.227
might be applied to the relationship itself.

319
00:13:48.694 --> 00:13:51.697
All that also gets added
to the relationship construction plan

320
00:13:52.097 --> 00:13:55.701
based on the relationship type.
Like the node labels,

321
00:13:55.701 --> 00:13:57.336
we expect the relationship types to be

322
00:13:57.336 --> 00:13:59.004
unique as we're doing this data import.

323
00:13:59.438 --> 00:14:02.908
Because this agent will be acting inside of a refinement loop,

324
00:14:03.242 --> 00:14:05.444
it might be necessary for it to actually

325
00:14:05.444 --> 00:14:07.913
remove a construction rule that it had done previously.

326
00:14:08.214 --> 00:14:10.816
So we're going to provide it a tool to do that

327
00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:13.519
both for the node construction and
also for relationship construction.

328
00:14:13.719 --> 00:14:15.721
This is the node version of it.

329
00:14:16.188 --> 00:14:19.692
You can define a similar tool
for removing relationship constructions as well.

330
00:14:19.992 --> 00:14:21.861
This will be based on the

331
00:14:21.861 --> 00:14:23.996
relationship type instead of the node label.

332
00:14:24.997 --> 00:14:27.700
The result of the agent's interacting with those tools

333
00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:31.003
for proposing construction rules and
then removing those construction rules

334
00:14:31.003 --> 00:14:32.805
is a complete construction plan.

335
00:14:33.072 --> 00:14:35.574
for taking some source data files of CSV files

336
00:14:35.574 --> 00:14:37.977
and creating a knowledge graph.

337
00:14:37.977 --> 00:14:40.079
So, if you let the LLM know

338
00:14:40.079 --> 00:14:42.681
what is the overall plan that it actually built,

339
00:14:42.681 --> 00:14:44.383
we have a tool for that as well.

